WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.860
It's impossible to imagine 
the long-awaited sequel to Blade Runner

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.720
without some version of the iconic music

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.920
that Vangelis composed for the original.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.080
That's because the Blade Runner score

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.220
is more than a score.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.500
It's an indispensable element of the movie.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.600
I mean, you could make that argument
 about almost all film music,

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.640
a movie minus its music

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:25.800
is not the same movie.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.760
That's true too, if you were to swap

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.880
one kind of music for another.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.320
[cheerful music playing]

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:39.800
In the video I did on

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.320
Howard Shore's incredible leitmotifs

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.220
in the Lord of the Rings,

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.840
I tried to show how the score 
helped guide and elevate

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.820
the emotional beats of the story.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.000
It's great work from a great composer, 
and hugely influential on the trilogy.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.100
I think Blade Runner's music is different,
it plays a different role.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.100
The score isn't laid 
on top of the visuals.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.440
It's not a guide, or an addition.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.140
The music is sort of baked into the DNA

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.720
of the movie itself.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.180
All the audio that you hear,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.360
including score, sound design, 
and dialogue

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.480
it's tightly integrated, 
each blurs into the others.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.300
This integration is really what separates Blade Runner from other science fiction films.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.960
After all, electronic music had been a staple 
of science fiction cinema

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.420
for three decades by then,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.100
going all the way back to Bernard Herrmann's 
use of the theremin

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.720
in "The Day the Earth Stood Still."

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.400
Herrmann and other revolutionary composers, 
like Louis and Bebe Barron,

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.620
used winding, bending, sputtering electronic sounds

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.380
to evoke aliens and alien planets.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.100
Blade Runner is somewhere in the middle

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.140
between the amelodic soundscapes
 of Forbidden Planet

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.100
and the grand, symphonic themes 
of something like Star Wars.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.260
Vangelis is pulling from that same toolbox 
of electronic sounds,

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.400
but these noises are put to use 
for a more musical purpose.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.740
In order to achieve that dream-like quality 
that the film has,

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.540
the audience shouldn't be able to identify

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.960
where the music ends and the world begins.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.980
The atmospherics that infuse this scene are 
a good example of what I'm talking about.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:23.100
They sound a little bit like wind chimes,
 something that could very well be in the space,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.420
and match pretty well with the dancing light 
on the walls.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.060
And yet, the sound is so layered 
with faint computer-y noises

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.860
that it could also be a machine 
somewhere in the room.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.600
It also serves as a kind of atmospheric bed from which

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.400
the clearly non-diegetic, 
not in-the-world strings rise

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:39.820
as Rachel enters.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.100
RACHEL: "Do you like our owl?"

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.560
Even the dialogue has a lot of reverb,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.980
giving it the same dreamy quality 
as the score and the sound effects.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:50.840
RACHEL: I'm Rachel.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:52.920
DECKARD: Deckard.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.680
Of course, reverberation is 
absolutely everywhere

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:56.600
in Blade Runner.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:01.680
You could even say that it's the manipulation of reverb that gives Blade Runner its distinct feeling.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.140
And though Vangelis is such a virtuoso at wielding it,

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.040
the digital reverb that you hear in this movie was something relatively new to music at the time.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.160
The world's first digital reverberation machine,
the EMT-250,

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.660
was released only six years before Blade Runner premiered in 1982.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.820
We all have a natural sense of reverb.
We hear it when we're in big spaces,

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:25.080
how our voice reverberates 
and decays across the room.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.060
Digital reverb uses algorithms 
to extend that decay even more.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.600
The Lexicon-224, which is what Vangelis used 
for Blade Runner, could extend decays

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.400
up to 70 seconds, allowing you to make spacey, 
ethereal sounds like this:

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:44.660
[SPACEY, ETHEREAL SOUND]

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.760
The emphasis on reverb accomplishes a few things:

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.520
as essayist Andrew Stiller noted,

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.800
"heavy reverb, because it implies a huge,

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.540
enclosed, hard-surface space, 
creates a mood of mystery and paranoia."

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.440
It also gives everything a feeling of distance,
as if the audio is coming from far away, like in a dream.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.180
Finally, reverberation blends things together.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.340
This is one of the crucial ways Vangelis

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.760
folds separate audio sources into one aural master track, because that is the goal here.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.440
Every scene is composed to create this cohesive acoustic environment.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.380
Sometimes, the emphasis is on dialogue 
or sound effects,

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.740
other times it's on the nostalgic music that calls back 
to classic film noir.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:26.840
[PAUSE]

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.020
Except that solo saxophone was never actually used 
in classic film noir,

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.720
which is a whole 'nother video; but anyway-

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:32.560
[RESUME]

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.620
Most of these scenes were interpreted
 by Vangelis on the spot.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.100
The way he composed the music for Blade Runner 
was by watching

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.100
the freshly edited scenes 
on a screen in his studio

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.580
and playing whatever they evoked 
in him at the time.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.280
He used primarily the Yamaha CS-80,

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.240
an analog synthesizer featured so prominently
in Blade Runner

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.180
that Vangelis has his own section 
in the CS-80 Wikipedia page.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.300
Even today, the CS-80 
is prized among audiophiles.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.520
It seems like anybody who ever writes about it 
describes it as

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.220
a living, breathing thing.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:06.600
That's partly down to the fact 
that the keys were pressure sensitive,

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.440
so the sound that it produced 
has a marvelous acoustic quality.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.200
In a way, it's the perfect instrument 
for Blade Runner.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.660
A machine, with the ability to match
 the emotional weight of the real thing.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.400
VANGELIS:  See, in one go, 
you can have from there...

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:22.260
[drum crash]

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:23.240
to there.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.100
[arpeggio]

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.980
It's different sound, 
to there, which is...

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:28.920
[synth]

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:32.540
different sound, 
to there, which is different again

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.540
[choral synth]

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.000
As much as possible, 
Vangelis used his spontaneous,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.360
first-tape compositions.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.960
And the result is a deeply personal
reaction to the images,

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.720
a second vision that fuses
with the director's own.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.500
Vangelis, like the great composer
 Edgard Varèse before him,

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:01.520
attempted to bring to life 
the complete sound profile of the city

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.680
interpreted through the genres 
of Blade Runner.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:09.240
Anybody who's lived in a large metropolis
knows that sound is a constant feature there.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.300
At night, you might hear a siren, 
or a scream somewhere in the distance.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:18.880
We may not have wheezing or sputtering machines,
but these days, we sure have a lot of beeping ones.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.240
Vangelis strings all these
disparate noises together

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.380
with musical themes 
that are stunning and appropriate.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.620
When the music swirls, 
it's as if it's coming

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.660
from within the characters themselves, 
like an internal cry or a release.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.960
Unfortunately, Vangelis isn't coming back 
for Blade Runner 2049.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.140
His job is going to Johann Johannsson,

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.480
a great composer who's worked 
with director Denis Villeneuve three times before.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:52.640
[EDIT: As of the release of the movie, the composers have changed to Hans Zimmer and Benjamin Wallfisch]

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.620
I don't envy him, I mean, 
everybody is going to be waiting

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.300
for him to play 
someone else's iconic music.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.520
If I were him, I'd resist the urge 
to mimic Vangelis completely.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.260
As much as I'd like to hear 
every classic note again.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:08.460
The sequel deserves music as personal 
and human as its original.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.700
I'm excited to hear Johann Johannsson's

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:16.120
interpretation of a world 
that so many of us are so eager to return to.

